// Spec model SKELETON — copy to docs/domain/models/<area>.als and replace the example domain.
//
// WHAT THIS IS: a checkable formal spec for ONE area of your domain. Each `.als` encodes the
// area's rules (invariants / policies) plus `check ... expect N` commands. Alloy exhaustively
// explores a small finite state space and fails (non-zero) when a guarantee unexpectedly gains
// or loses a counterexample — catching a rule-level regression at the DESIGN stage, before any
// code exists to test. This sits one sieve "left" of your tests; see docs/spec-rules.md.
//
// HOW IT RUNS: `sh scripts/check-domain-model.sh` (advisory by default — skips cleanly if the
// Alloy toolchain is unavailable; a real counterexample is the only thing that fails it).
//
// FILE-HEADER CONVENTION (fill these in for your area — they make the model self-documenting):
//   - Purpose:        which area / rule-doc this formalizes (e.g. docs/spec/<area>.md).
//   - Rule IDs:       the R-<n> ids this model reflects (1 rule = 2 reflections: a test + this assert).
//   - Implementation: the source files this model MIRRORS (the model is the spec, not the code under
//                     test — list the files so a reader can cross-check, e.g. src/.../Foo.<ext>).
//   - Run:            scripts/check-domain-model.sh
//
// ---- EXAMPLE (delete and replace with your domain) ----
// Purpose:        formalize docs/spec/order.md state rules.
// Rule IDs:       R-1 (an item leaves Open exactly once), R-2 (Cancelled is terminal).
// Implementation: src/domain/Order.<ext> (transition()).
// Run:            scripts/check-domain-model.sh

module example

// ---- Vocabulary: the finite set of states/values of this area (one enum or sig) ----
// Replace with your domain's states. An enum is the typical shape for a closed state set.
enum State { Open, Closed, Cancelled }

// A thing that holds a state. Add the fields your invariant needs (kept minimal here).
sig Item {
  state: one State
}

// ---- Derived vocabulary (optional): name a meaningful subset, the way code names a predicate ----
// Terminal states: once here, no further transition is allowed (used by the invariant below).
fun terminalStates: set State { Closed + Cancelled }

// ---- Invariant (R-1): the condition the area must ALWAYS satisfy ----
// Phrase the predicate in domain terms; this is the model shadow of the implementation test.
// EXAMPLE rule: "every item is in exactly one of the known states" (trivially true here — replace
// with the real constraint your area enforces, e.g. relating two fields, or a multiplicity).
pred itemStateIsWellFormed[i: Item] {
  one i.state
}

// ---- Assertion + check: ask Alloy to PROVE the invariant holds for all small instances ----
// `expect 0` = "there should be NO counterexample". If a future edit lets the invariant be
// violated, Alloy finds an instance and the check fails (non-zero) — the regression signal.
// Map the assert name to a rule ID in a comment so rule -> test -> assert stays traceable.
assert ItemStateIsAlwaysWellFormed {        // R-1
  all i: Item | itemStateIsWellFormed[i]
}
check ItemStateIsAlwaysWellFormed for 5 expect 0

// ---- (Optional) An INTENDED counterexample: `expect 1` documents a deliberate gap ----
// The reason is MANDATORY (check-intended-counterexample enforces it). Turning `expect 0` into
// `expect 1` gives up a guarantee; letting that through unexplained lets a converge-to-green loop
// pass off a rule regression as "design intent". Put `intended: <why>` on the same line or the
// line above.
// Use this to assert that a property does NOT hold by design (e.g. a deliberately non-injective
// fail-safe mapping). It documents intent so a reviewer does not "fix" it. Delete if unused.
//
// assert SomethingThatShouldNotHold { ... }
// check SomethingThatShouldNotHold for 5 expect 1  // intended: <why this gap is deliberate>
